          </div>

          <p>
            The split is obvious once you see the protein column. A scoop of peanut butter whey gives three to four times the protein of a serving of powdered peanut butter. PBfit and PB2 are excellent at what they do, which is delivering peanut flavor with far less fat than regular peanut butter, but they are not a substitute for a protein powder if your goal is 25 g of protein in a shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best approach for many people is to use both: a peanut butter or chocolate peanut butter whey for the protein, and a tablespoon or two of PBfit or PB2 to deepen the flavor. That combination is how many buyers get a shake that actually tastes like peanut butter, because most flavored powders are milder than the jar.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="powder-vs-pb" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Peanut Butter Protein Powder vs Powdered Peanut Butter Like PBfit
          </h2>

          <p>
            Powdered peanut butter is made by pressing most of the oil out of roasted peanuts and grinding what is left into flour. PBfit's label lists peanut flour, coconut palm sugar and salt; PB2 Original lists roasted peanuts, sugar and salt. Per two tablespoons, retail listings put PBfit at roughly 50 to 70 calories with about 6 to 8 g of protein depending on the version and serving weight, and PB2's comparison table shows 60 calories and 6 g of protein per 13 g serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare that with regular peanut butter, which runs around 190 calories, 7 to 8 g of protein and about 16 g of fat for two tablespoons. Powdered peanut butter keeps most of the protein and cuts roughly two-thirds of the calories by removing fat. That is a good trade for flavoring oats, smoothies and sauces, but the protein density is still modest: roughly 10 g of protein per 100 calories, against about 20 g per 100 calories for a whey isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            PB2 Performance sits between the two categories. The Pure Peanut Protein version is a single ingredient, roasted peanuts with more of the fat removed, and its label lists 22 g of plant protein in 200 calories per two-scoop, 45 g serving. That makes it a genuine protein powder, but a calorie-dense one: about 11 g of protein per 100 calories, closer to powdered peanut butter than to whey. Peanut protein is also lower in the amino acid methionine than whey, so it works best as part of a varied diet rather than as your only protein source.
          </p>

          <p>
            So which should you buy? If you want a post-workout shake or a high-protein snack, buy a peanut butter flavored whey or plant protein. If you want peanut flavor with fewer calories in oatmeal, smoothies or baking, buy powdered peanut butter. If you want a peanut-based protein with no sweeteners or dairy, PB2 Performance is the only pick here that fits, as long as the higher calories suit your goal. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">guide to how much protein you need</a> helps work out a daily target.
          </p>
        </div>

