        <div id="whats-inside" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Makes a Peanut Butter Protein Powder: Real Peanuts or Flavoring?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most peanut butter protein powders contain real peanut, and the ingredient list tells you how much. Quest's Peanut Butter label lists its whey isolate, micellar casein and milk protein isolate blend, then peanuts, then natural flavors that also contain peanuts. Dymatize ISO100 Chocolate Peanut Butter lists partially defatted peanut flour third, after the two whey isolates and before alkalized cocoa. Transparent Labs lists peanut flour, with a retailer panel showing about 3 g of organic peanut butter powder per 37 g scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Peanut flour adds genuine roasted flavor and a little fat and carbohydrate, which is why peanut butter flavors often run a few calories higher than the same brand's vanilla. Orgain's plant version lists organic roasted peanut flour alongside its pea, brown rice and chia blend. Ghost's Nutter Butter flavor is licensed from the cookie brand; check its ingredient list and allergen statement for peanut and wheat on the tub you buy, as licensed cookie flavors can include cookie pieces.
          </p>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition's Gold Standard Chocolate Peanut Butter is the exception we found. The US label listings we checked state that it contains milk and soy, not peanut, which suggests the peanut taste comes from flavoring. That makes it milder than tubs with peanut flour, according to buyers. It does not make it suitable for anyone with a peanut allergy, because shared manufacturing facilities and label changes are always possible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chocolate peanut butter is the most common version of the flavor, and the cocoa matters. Cocoa is a major source of cadmium and lead in protein powder, a topic we cover in detail in our <a href="/best-chocolate-protein-powder/">best chocolate protein powder</a> guide. Plain peanut butter flavors such as Quest's avoid cocoa entirely. If you drink protein every day, alternating chocolate peanut butter with a plain peanut butter or <a href="/best-vanilla-protein-powder/">vanilla protein powder</a> keeps that exposure lower.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="allergy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Peanut Allergy Warning: Read Every Label
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">ALLERGY WARNING</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Not safe for anyone with a peanut allergy</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Quest, ISO100, Transparent Labs, Orgain, PBfit and PB2 all list peanut as an ingredient. Even flavors without peanut on the label can be made in facilities that process peanuts. If you or anyone you share food with has a peanut allergy, avoid this category and follow the advice of your allergist.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Peanut is one of the major food allergens US law requires manufacturers to declare. On every product here that contains peanut, it appears in the contains statement, for example Quest's label reads contains milk, peanuts and soy, and ISO100 Chocolate Peanut Butter reads contains milk, peanut and soy. Read that line, not the flavor name, because a name can be misleading in both directions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cross-contact is the second risk. Many protein brands make peanut and non-peanut flavors on the same equipment, and labels often carry a statement such as processed in a facility that also processes peanuts. Quest's non-peanut flavors carry exactly that statement. These precautionary statements are voluntary in the US, so the absence of one does not prove a product is free of traces. People with severe allergies should ask the manufacturer directly about shared lines.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shared kitchens matter too. Powdered peanut butter is a fine dust that spreads easily onto counters, scoops and blender jars. If someone in the household has a peanut allergy, keep peanut powders separate and wash equipment thoroughly. This guide is not medical advice; anyone with a diagnosed food allergy, or a child with suspected allergies, should talk to an allergist or registered dietitian before introducing new products. If you need a peanut-free option, our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">protein powder for sensitive stomachs</a> guide covers simpler formulas, but always confirm allergen statements yourself.
          </p>
        </div>

