              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Powdered peanut butter gives about 6 to 8 g of protein per two tablespoons, roughly a quarter of a whey scoop. It is a great flavor ingredient, but if you are relying on it to hit a protein target, you will fall well short. Pair it with a protein powder instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the flavor name on allergies</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Some peanut butter flavors use peanut flour, some use flavoring, and non-peanut flavors can still be made on shared lines. Read the contains statement and any facility warning on every tub, and contact the brand if anyone in your home has a peanut allergy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Copying numbers from another flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Quest's peanut butter has 23 g of protein per scoop, its salted caramel 26 g and its cookies and cream 20 g. Peanut flour and cocoa also shift calories. Always read the panel for the exact flavor, not the brand's best-seller.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drinking chocolate peanut butter twice a day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chocolate peanut butter combines cocoa with peanut, and cocoa is a major source of cadmium and lead in protein powder. Alternate with a plain peanut butter or vanilla flavor if you drink protein daily, rather than relying on one cocoa-based tub.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
